patfile/: pattern files for features of the pattern language
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
arrindex.axx                        the array-symbol features: bare names as
                                    items, a name used as a subscript, and
                                    .index (manual 3.6.1)
symcap.axx                          the `!Y<set>[<var>]` symbol capture: reads
                                    one item name of a set and binds its index
                                    to a variable, so one set's spelling can be
                                    rewritten into another's (manual 3.6.3)
echo.axx                            `.echo` written on a body line: strings
                                    and pattern-layer expressions printed to
                                    stderr once per source line, emitting no
                                    words (manual 3.14.1)
passthru.axx                        .passthru and .eol: lines that match no
                                    pattern are let through as text, one
                                    output line per source line (manual 3.16,
                                    3.17)
datadir.axx                         the source data directives .db / .dw / .dd
                                    / .dq / .do / .dy / .dz: 8-bit words,
                                    little-endian, with .eol (manual 5.3)
datadirbe.axx                       the same with 8-bit words, big-endian
datadirw.axx                        the same with 16-bit words
textmode.axx                        .textmode, the `!L` expression / label
                                    capture and `{{.exp(x)}}`: text
                                    replacement keeping labels, expressions,
                                    `;` comments and the leading indentation
                                    spelled as written (manual 3.18)
expcap.axx                          `{{.exp(x)}}` on every capture: a
                                    lowercase variable, `!`, `!!`, `!F` / `!D`
                                    / `!Q`, `!E`, `!S{{name}}`, `!L` and `!Y`
                                    all keep the source spelling along with
                                    the value (manual 3.18)
exprmiss.axx                        captures with no expression or factor:
                                    `!`, `!!`, `!L` and `!F` / `!D` / `!Q` do
                                    not match an empty operand or one whose
                                    factor is missing (`1+`, `-`, `~`, `()`),
                                    so those lines are let through by
                                    .passthru (manual 3.3)
ministr.axx                         strings in the mini language: `.call
                                    f(10,"text")`, `+` `*` and comparisons,
                                    byte subscripts and slices, `.str` /
                                    `.chr` / `.int`, `.emit` / `.echo` of a
                                    string, `.call f(x,.exp(x))` passing a
                                    pattern variable's captured text, a string
                                    symbol passed by its name, arrays holding
                                    strings, `.for x in <array>`, rewriting a
                                    byte with `s[i] = v`, `/*` and `::` inside
                                    strings, `.emit` of an array, an array
                                    symbol passed by its name, and replacing a
                                    range with `x[lo:hi] = v` (MINI.md)
endsub.axx                          closing a `.sub` block with `.endsub`
                                    instead of `.return`: the two spellings
                                    mean the same thing and can be mixed in
                                    one pattern file, including for tables
                                    referred to from another table (manual
                                    3.7.2)
unordered.axx                       .unordered: directives that hold for the
                                    whole file wherever they are written, and
                                    .map tables per variable, so the Z80 C is
                                    a register for one variable and carry for
                                    another (manual 3.19)

